The whole tree renders before anything is written, so a broken seed anywhere aborts the run
with no partial result: the error names the offending node and the receipt is empty, which
is what a run that wrote a file could never produce — every write prints a line.

-- fixtures/xdg/booping/config.yaml --
core:
  e2e_demo:
    tree:
      ok.txt: "fine\n"
      bad.txt: "{% if %}"
-- cmd --
booping scaffold core.e2e_demo.tree out
-- exit --
1
-- stdout --
-- stderr --
error: core.e2e_demo.tree.bad.txt: Jinja error in seed content: [..]
